12.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: J is the intervention that is most directly targeted to the valve-related complication risk that is created by heart valve surgery.

A prosthetic valve is thrombogenic.  Systemic thrombo-embolism and prosthetic valve thrombosis are the classic early post-operative complications after valve replacement/repair and are prevented by post-operative anticoagulation. With a mechanical prosthesis anticoagulation is life-long and with a bioprosthesis anticoagulation is usually required for the early post-operative period. Pulse 60, BP 120/80, RR 16 and alert x3 are reassuring but they do not remove the risk of valve thrombosis / stroke.

In practice discharge planning after cardiac valve surgery is multimodal and would also include:
* safe mobilisation / physical therapy to prevent DVT / atelectasis / deconditioning;
* adequate analgesia for effective coughing and mobilization;
* patient education on activity restriction and wound care and a timely post-operative review, usually 1-2 weeks not one month, before return to normal daily routine;
* heart-healthy diet and risk factor modification.

Encouraging “regular exercise to increase circulation” and allowing an immediate return to a normal daily routine on the basis of the patient feeling well is inappropriate in the immediate post-operative period. Asymptomatic status does not eliminate the need for prophylaxis of valve thrombosis.

